"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
from teaching.foundations import put

put(14, '''An order service calls a payment provider. The provider may succeed while the order service cannot receive its reply. Different machines can keep working, stop, or lose communication independently, so there is no single local observation that explains everything. ¶ Distributed design specifies what outcomes are allowed despite this uncertainty. Begin with clear state owners and operations, then introduce additional copies or coordination for a real requirement. More nodes add possible failure combinations alongside capacity. The design therefore needs a protocol: rules for messages, state changes, acknowledgments, and recovery. A diagram showing connected machines does not supply those rules on its own. || One logical operation / Several independently failing participants / Caller has incomplete information / Protocol defines allowed outcomes

The provider charges a card, but the order service loses the reply. The local order may still be pending while the remote effect already exists. This is a partial failure: some of the overall operation happened and another part did not complete as observed. ¶ Keep “not attempted” separate from “attempted, outcome unknown.” Record an operation or provider reference and reconcile the status. A local rollback cannot erase the remote charge. Compensation is possible only where the business and provider support a suitable further action. Treating every exception as a definite clean failure can lead to resubmitting an effect that already succeeded. || Provider accepts payment / Order service loses response / Payment state is uncertain locally / Reconciliation establishes actual outcome

A heartbeat does not arrive. The other machine might have failed, or its message might simply be delayed. For a time, these cases can look identical to the observer. ¶ A timeout is a practical suspicion and a limit on waiting, not proof that the old process stopped. A retry can overlap the delayed original request. Bound waiting to protect resources, while using a protocol to prevent incompatible owners or duplicate effects. State which operations must wait or reject when authority is uncertain. A short timer can improve responsiveness, but correctness must also hold when its suspicion is wrong. || Heartbeat delayed / Observer suspects failure / Old participant may still run / Protocol fences conflicting authority

Suppose a protocol assumes machines either follow its rules or crash. A participant that lies about its stored history is outside that assumption. A fault model states the failures the design claims to tolerate. ¶ Common models include crash-stop, crash-recovery, message loss, partitions, and arbitrary Byzantine behaviour. Recovery also depends on whether required persistent state survives a restart. State these conditions before calculating a replica count. Three copies on one failed device have a different failure exposure from independently placed durable copies. Correctness reasoning is meaningful only for the failure cases and resource assumptions it actually covers. || Choose crash or Byzantine model / State storage-survival assumptions / Define network uncertainty / Evaluate protocol only within model

Two machines stamp events using their own clocks. Clock drift or adjustment can make the later causal event appear earlier by timestamp. A wall-clock comparison therefore does not necessarily establish what caused what. ¶ Use an appropriate monotonic clock for elapsed durations where available. For expiry, identify the authority for time and the uncertainty the rule permits. An ordering requirement may need a sequence or protocol log rather than client timestamps. Recording source time and observed time separately helps explain delayed events. The design must distinguish user-facing dates, measured durations, and causal relationships instead of asking one timestamp to establish all three. || Node A clock says 10:01 / Node B clock says 10:00 / Messages can still flow A to B / Clock order does not establish cause

A physical clock estimates real time. A logical clock records ordering information and need not count seconds. A logical counter can tell us something about event relationships without saying what time of day they occurred. ¶ Use the kind of time appropriate to the question. Displaying a date and ordering protocol actions are different tasks. Even subtracting timestamps from two machines can be misleading if their clock errors are unknown. A combined physical/logical scheme needs its own specified rules. Name which meaning each stored field has so later code does not interpret an ordering counter as a duration or a timestamp as proof of causality. || Physical clock estimates time / Logical clock tracks ordering / Different questions need different clocks / Synchronization error bounds interpretation

A process with Lamport counter 5 receives a message carrying 9. It advances to max(5,9)+1 = 10. Local increments and these receive rules ensure that a causal predecessor has a smaller counter. A smaller counter alone does not prove causation. ¶ Vector clocks keep progress for several participants. Vectors [1,0] and [0,1] each contain progress absent from the other, so they represent concurrent versions under the model. This can reveal conflicts but costs metadata. Neither clock decides how to merge two account changes or independently provides a global real-time commit order. The clock describes a relation; the application still needs a valid resolution rule. || A event has vector [1,0] / B independent event has [0,1] / Neither vector dominates / Merge policy must handle concurrency

A user writes a post, reads it, then switches replicas. What may the next read show? A consistency model specifies which observation histories the system permits. ¶ Write examples before choosing a setting: can a read return an older value, can two readers see different orders, and must a reply appear after its parent post? Different objects can need different rules. A feed count may tolerate delay while an allocation requires one winner. Stronger observations can require more coordination or fewer available serving paths during a partition. Name the model and its operation scope; a bare word such as “strong” leaves important assumptions unstated. || User writes post P / Another region reads / Possible absence or presence / Chosen consistency contract defines legality

Replicas may briefly disagree after an update, then converge once updates stop and communication and repair succeed. Eventual consistency expresses convergence under such assumptions. It does not by itself specify a maximum delay or decide how conflicts are merged. ¶ “Strong consistency” is often used for linearizable behaviour, but check what the speaker means. A social feed may allow propagation delay while the author still needs to read their own post immediately. State these per-operation contracts. Delayed convergence does not excuse losing an acknowledged update outside the agreed failure policy. Freshness, conflict handling, and durability need their own precise rules. || Write arrives in region A / Region B initially lacks it / Replication and repair proceed / Convergence depends on stated assumptions

A write of 2 finishes before another client begins a read. For a linearizable register, that read cannot return the superseded value 1 unless an intervening write permits it. Each operation behaves as if it took effect at one point between invocation and response, respecting real-time precedence. ¶ Overlapping operations can have more than one valid ordering. A timed-out operation can still take effect because the client never obtained a definite completed result. Achieving linearizability requires a suitable protocol for writes and reads. Majority arithmetic and timestamps alone leave version choice, concurrency, and recovery rules unresolved. || Write 2 completes / Later read begins / Read must respect completed write / Overlapping operations permit legal order choices

Two database transactions can be equivalent to a serial order without that order necessarily following the real-time order observed by separate clients. Serializability concerns whole transactions and their equivalent serial execution. ¶ Linearizability concerns real-time ordering of operations. Strict serializability combines transaction serialization with real-time precedence. Distinguish these when combining transactions and replica reads. Also identify the actual atomic boundary: a serializable local transaction does not bring an external payment provider into the same transaction. The property helps reason about work within its scope; messages and remote effects still need their own ordering and recovery design. || Transaction history has serial equivalent / Real-time precedence is separate / Strict serializability adds that constraint / External effects remain outside local transaction

Sequential consistency allows one common ordering that preserves each participant's program order, without necessarily preserving real-time order across participants. Causal consistency preserves dependencies while permitting unrelated concurrent events to appear in different orders. ¶ Consider a reply written only after its author read a post. A causally consistent view should not expose that dependent reply without the parent information it depends on. The system needs dependency tracking or a supported protocol to provide this observation. Ordinary delayed replication alone does not establish it. Use a concrete event history to decide which order matters, rather than requiring a total order for every unrelated action. || User sees post P / User writes reply R to P / Another replica shows R / It must satisfy required causal dependency on P

After editing a profile, a user expects the next read to include that edit. Read-your-writes provides this session guarantee. After seeing version ten, the user also may expect not to see version nine later; that is monotonic reads. ¶ Routing, replication-position tracking, or another suitable protocol can support these rules. They do not necessarily provide global linearizability. Decide how progress follows a session when the user changes devices or an instance fails. Sticky routing alone may lose the guarantee at failover. A read might need to wait, use a current owner, or return an explicit unavailability result rather than silently go backward. || Session observes version 10 / Next request routes elsewhere / New owner must reach required progress / Do not return earlier version 9

Two groups of replicas cannot communicate during a partition. If both must answer every request, they cannot always preserve CAP's formal atomic-consistency requirement for the shared read/write object. The impossibility concerns those defined properties under the specified partition model. ¶ CAP is therefore not a permanent menu of any two desirable features. Decide what each operation does when coordination is unavailable. Exclusive allocation may reject or wait; a different operation may serve an explicitly weaker view. State the exact property rather than equating formal availability with an ordinary uptime percentage. When the network is functioning, latency and coordination still require separate analysis. || Replicas separated by partition / One side accepts a write / Other side cannot know that write / Serving all reads conflicts with atomic visibility

A write can wait for a distant replica even when the network is healthy. That improves some coordination possibilities but adds travel time. PACELC draws attention to this ordinary latency/consistency trade-off as well as CAP's partition case. ¶ The mnemonic asks: during a partition, what availability/consistency choice is made; else, what latency/consistency trade-off is made? Use it as a review lens rather than a substitute for the protocol. Different operations and configurations can take different paths. Quantify the distances and waits with stated assumptions instead of assigning one permanent letter label to a product. || Partition behavior decision / Normal-operation coordination decision / Geographic acknowledgment adds delay / Operation-specific contracts guide the choice

With three fixed replicas, a two-member read set and a two-member write set must overlap. More generally, R+W>N gives read/write intersection, and W>N/2 gives intersection between write quorums. ¶ That is a statement about sets. A reader still needs a correct way to interpret the versions it receives, and writers need ordering, persistence, and concurrency rules. Membership changes or substitute nodes can invalidate a naive fixed-set argument. In N=3,R=2,W=2, at least one queried participant belongs to the completed write set, but that observation alone is not a linearizability proof. Keep the arithmetic and protocol claims separate. || Replicas: A, B, C / Write set: A and B / Read set: B and C / Intersection B is necessary evidence, not full protocol

An old leader pauses. Another leader is elected, but the old process later resumes and tries to write. If both are treated as authorities, the system has split brain. ¶ A missing heartbeat cannot physically stop the earlier owner. An ownership protocol must be paired with enforcement where effects are accepted. For example, a storage receiver can reject requests carrying an older fencing epoch after it has accepted a newer one. Expiring only a local lock variable leaves the paused process able to act elsewhere. Test delayed messages and resumed workers as well as clean crashes, because these histories expose the stale-owner problem. || Old leader pauses / New leader obtains higher epoch / Old leader resumes / Resource rejects stale epoch effects

Some decisions need agreement: who owns a partition, which configuration is current, or what order metadata changes follow. Distributed coordination establishes such shared decisions under a failure protocol. ¶ Use it where the invariant needs it. Independent updates that safely commute may not require a shared round on every operation. The coordinator also needs recovery and failure rules; a service name does not make it reliable. Decide how clients behave when it is unavailable. Reusing safe cached configuration can differ from issuing new exclusive ownership. Separating these activities helps preserve useful service without granting incompatible authority. || Need one authoritative decision / Consensus metadata records epoch / Clients act under that epoch / Resource enforces accepted authority

A design may quietly assume every network call succeeds, arrives instantly, costs nothing, and reaches an unchanged destination. The fallacies of distributed computing collect warnings about such assumptions, including bandwidth, security, infrastructure diversity, and multiple administrators. ¶ Turn each warning into a concrete review question. What is the deadline? Who controls the other endpoint? How is the message versioned and authorised? How much data does fifty-way fan-out transfer? These questions uncover hidden requirements. The list is an engineering aid rather than a protocol guarantee. Its value comes from applying the concerns to real paths and failure histories. || Locate remote boundary / Account for latency and loss / Verify security and version assumptions / Assign ownership across administrative boundaries

“No seat is sold to two buyers” excludes a bad outcome: it is a safety property. “Every accepted booking eventually resolves” asks for progress, a liveness obligation that needs stated environmental assumptions. ¶ Rejecting every request can preserve the first property while providing no useful progress. Conversely, replying immediately can appear responsive while breaking exclusive ownership. Write both requirements and identify which failures may delay completion. Some network and failure models prevent unconditional progress guarantees. Expose stuck work so operators can restore progress without inventing a second winner or losing an already accepted operation. || Safety: no double allocation / Liveness: accepted booking resolves / Partition may require waiting / Recovery must preserve both obligations''')

put(15, '''A primary accepts the writes for a dataset and replicas keep copies. One current authority can simplify conflict handling, but the topology needs rules for replacing that authority and preventing an old primary from returning as a competing writer. ¶ State when a write is acknowledged and which failures preserve it. Replica reads may show earlier data. “One primary, two replicas” describes placement, not a complete consensus protocol. Persistence, election eligibility, membership, and recovery determine the actual guarantee. Ask what happens to an acknowledged write if the primary disappears before its newest changes reach another eligible owner. || Primary owns writes / Replicas receive changes / Acknowledgment policy defines durability / Promotion must preserve authorized history

A leader sends an ordered stream of updates to followers. A follower may receive bytes, persist them, and later apply them to its local state. These milestones are not necessarily simultaneous. ¶ Track the positions relevant to the implementation. Receipt in memory may not provide crash-recovery protection. A follower serving reads may still be behind even after receiving recent records. Synchronous and asynchronous arrangements choose different acknowledgment points and waits. Promotion needs a rule about which history is safe to continue. Understanding these stages makes lag and durability claims more precise than saying that a follower “has the data.” || Leader appends record / Follower receives it / Required persistence is acknowledged / Apply progress controls readable state

Two regions accept writes locally while their connection is interrupted. On reconnection, both may have changed the same record. Multi-leader replication needs a resolution rule for those concurrent updates. ¶ Last-writer-wins can converge by choosing a timestamp, but clock differences or overwriting can discard meaningful work. Convergence alone does not establish a correct business merge. Use multiple writers where conflict semantics fit the domain, or add coordination for rules requiring one winner. Include deletes, prolonged disconnection, and replication-loop prevention in the design. Local write latency is a benefit only when the resulting observations and conflicts remain acceptable. || Region A accepts edit / Region B accepts concurrent edit / Both versions propagate / Explicit merge or conflict resolution decides result

A client contacts several replicas and compares their versions instead of sending every write through one permanent leader. Leaderless designs use protocols of this kind, with choices about read repair, hinted handoff, and background anti-entropy. ¶ Each mechanism solves part of the problem. Quorum counts still need correct version interpretation, membership, and concurrency rules. Test two concurrent writes and a stale replica returning after a long failure. Choose the arrangement for its actual availability and conflict contract. Removing a permanent leader does not remove every need for coordination, nor does two replies out of three automatically establish linearizability. || Coordinator contacts replicas / Responses carry versions / Reconcile under documented rules / Background repair restores appropriate copies

A primary can reply immediately and copy later, or wait for specified replica evidence. Asynchronous replication chooses the earlier reply. If the only current copy is then lost, a promoted replica may lack recent acknowledged writes. ¶ Synchronous replication waits, but the word needs detail: does a replica acknowledge receipt, durable storage, or application? Waiting for independent durable copies can strengthen protection within the failure model, while adding latency and possible blocking. State which milestone success promises and test it during failover. The configuration is part of the application's data-loss contract, rather than an operating detail detached from users. || Write reaches leader / Required replica acknowledgment defined / Caller receives success / Failure loss depends on that boundary

In a fixed three-node group, any two-member majority overlaps another two-member majority. This can carry evidence from one decision into a later one, provided the shared participant follows the protocol. ¶ A quorum is an allowed decision set, not simply a vote count. Members may need to persist a vote or accepted record before replying and obey later restrictions based on it. Election eligibility and membership transitions also matter. Without these rules, intersecting sets can still acknowledge incompatible histories. Use quorum arithmetic to understand a reviewed protocol, rather than treating it as a complete algorithm for consistent storage. || Majority A+B / Majority B+C / Node B intersects / Persistent voting and log rules preserve evidence

Suppose replicas agree on the sequence “create task 7, then complete task 7.” If each applies the same commands deterministically from the same state, they can maintain equivalent task state. This is the replicated state-machine approach. ¶ Consensus supplies agreement under its stated fault and progress assumptions. Values such as random identifiers or current time must be part of an agreed command or otherwise handled consistently; recomputing them independently can make replicas differ. Also separate state-machine application from external effects. Applying one command on several replicas must not send several unprotected charges. External delivery needs explicit ownership, duplicate handling, and recovery outside the agreed log boundary. || Consensus orders command C / Replicas apply C deterministically / States evolve equivalently / External effects need separate coordination

Raft organises agreement around a leader and a replicated log. A term identifies an election generation. A candidate needs a majority of votes and a sufficiently up-to-date log under Raft's comparison rules. Votes and required log state are persisted as specified by the protocol. ¶ The leader replicates entries and advances its commit position. It can directly use majority replication to commit a current-term entry; earlier entries become committed through the protocol's ordering rules. Counting copies of an arbitrary older entry is insufficient. This distinction preserves history across leadership changes. Reads also need a supported freshness mechanism. The paper's rules work together; this explanation is not a replacement implementation. || Election term advances / Majority selects eligible leader / Leader replicates ordered entries / Current-term commit rule protects history

Paxos uses numbered proposals and intersecting groups to preserve an agreed choice. During preparation, a proposer gathers promises and any relevant earlier accepted value. It must carry the required value forward when it asks for acceptance. ¶ The key idea is preserving prior evidence, rather than allowing each new leader to choose freely. Multi-Paxos uses related decisions to build an ordered sequence, often reducing repeated preparation around a stable leader. This is a conceptual introduction. Persistence, changing membership, retries, and progress under contention need precise rules. Use a reviewed implementation for real coordination instead of turning the introductory voting picture into production lock code. || Higher proposal prepares / Majority reports accepted evidence / Proposer preserves required value / Majority acceptance chooses under protocol

A record can appear in one replica's log without being committed. A node can also have committed records it has not applied yet. Ordering, commitment, durable storage, and application describe different progress. ¶ Recovery must retain the committed history while handling incompatible uncommitted suffixes. Reply according to the protocol's actual acceptance rule, rather than because bytes reached some follower. Monitor commit and apply progress, and ensure snapshot installation represents a compatible history. These distinctions explain why a local log inspection alone may not establish what the application is entitled to expose as an accepted result. || Log entry appended / Entry replicated but not yet committed / Protocol establishes committed prefix / State machine applies committed history

Suppose an old cluster and a new cluster have disjoint decision majorities. Replacing membership abruptly can let both make incompatible decisions. A supported membership transition prevents that gap. ¶ Raft's joint-consensus approach temporarily requires the relevant majorities from both configurations. New nodes can first catch up without voting. Use the implementation's reconfiguration procedure, preserving persistent identities and testing interruption during the change. Increasing a cluster-size setting is not merely adding capacity: it changes which groups have authority. The safety argument must remain valid at the transition, not only before and after it. || Old voter configuration / Controlled overlapping transition / New members catch up / New configuration becomes authoritative

Worker A has token 7 and pauses. Its lease expires; B obtains token 8. A later resumes and sends an old write. The receiving resource must reject that stale authority. ¶ A fencing token is an ordered ownership epoch checked at the effect boundary. The receiver must compare the token and apply the protected action atomically under its rule. Issuing a token that storage never checks does not fence a worker. Also account for effects already accepted before the newer owner took over. Fencing blocks later stale actions; it does not undo earlier accepted ones or automatically protect an unrelated external API. || Worker A holds epoch 7 / Worker B obtains epoch 8 / Store records highest accepted epoch / Later epoch 7 effect is rejected

Missing heartbeats suggest failure, but a long pause or congested network can produce the same observation. Failure detectors trade responsiveness against false suspicions according to timing assumptions. ¶ Shorter intervals can begin recovery sooner while increasing unnecessary suspicion. Keep this tuning separate from the protocol that transfers authority and fences stale owners. Safety should hold even if a suspicion is wrong. A received heartbeat also does not establish that every request can meet its deadline, so readiness and load signals can still be useful. Detection provides evidence; ownership rules determine which participant may act on it. || Heartbeat expected / Delay crosses suspicion threshold / Election or failover may start / Safety survives an incorrect suspicion

A metadata service can maintain current ownership epochs or configuration through a strongly consistent protocol. These are usually small control decisions, rather than the full high-volume application payload. ¶ Identify which actions need a fresh decision and which can use safe cached information. During a metadata outage, existing traffic might continue while new leases or deployments must wait. Drawing this control/data distinction makes the failure policy clearer. Avoid placing every request behind a synchronous control-plane lookup without a reason. The control service's own capacity, fault assumptions, and recovery are still dependencies of the decisions it owns. || Small metadata decision / Consensus preserves authoritative epoch / Data plane uses cached safe state / New decisions pause when authority unavailable

A lock grants permission to a named owner. A lease makes that permission time-bounded under particular timing rules. The holder can pause past expiry and later continue unless the protected resource recognises that its authority is stale. ¶ Use resource-side fencing where that history threatens correctness. Release operations must check ownership, so an old holder cannot delete a newer owner's lock. Acquisition and the later protected action are separate events with a gap between them. For an invariant entirely inside one database, a local transaction may avoid unnecessary distributed locking. Choose coordination for the actual shared resource and failure history. || Acquire lease with owner identity / Perform bounded protected work / Lease expires or owner pauses / Resource rejects stale owner through fencing

A grow-only set can merge copies by union: each addition remains present, and merging in different orders converges. CRDTs design types around update and merge rules with suitable algebraic and delivery assumptions. ¶ More complex types need metadata. An observed-remove set distinguishes additions known at removal from concurrent additions. Convergence does not enforce an arbitrary rule such as nonnegative stock. Choose semantics that match the domain, and preserve the metadata needed for safe merge and cleanup. Some business edits are better handled by explicit conflict review. An automatic convergent answer is useful only if that answer means the right thing. || Replica A adds item X / Replica B adds item Y / Merge by union yields X,Y / Domain invariants still require separate reasoning

A local asynchronous reply and a cross-region durable quorum expose different latency and possible-loss behaviour. Regional replication must state where writes are authoritative, which copies acknowledgment requires, and how conflicts or promotion work. ¶ During a partition, some operations may reject while permitted stale reads continue. On reconnection, an old primary must catch up or reconcile before it is trusted to write again. Test failback as well as failover. Restoring traffic quickly can conceal a second failure if stale authority or history returns after the visible outage. The regional design includes that return path, not merely the initial switch. || Regions exchange history / Partition changes reachable authority / Chosen policy governs writes / Recovery and failback reconcile histories''')

put(16, '''Checkout can save an order and let receipt delivery happen later. Synchronous communication makes the caller wait for a result; asynchronous communication separates submission from observing completion. ¶ The delayed work still needs an owner. If accepting an order promises a receipt workflow, record the required intent durably instead of leaving it only in the request's memory. A queue can absorb a burst, but continued arrivals above worker capacity still build a backlog. State what the initial response means and where clients or operators can see progress after the initiating request ends. || Order transaction commits / Receipt intent becomes durable / Request can return / Worker later records delivery outcome

A queue commonly distributes jobs among consumers. A retained log keeps an append history that consumers can replay. Pub/sub delivers publications to subscribers according to a particular retention and acknowledgment policy. ¶ Products combine these behaviours, so read their actual contract. A subscriber absent during an ephemeral publication may never receive it. A consumer that must recover after a long outage needs enough retention and a stored progress position. Choose by recovery and ordering requirements, rather than assuming that every broker is a durable history. The important question is which records remain and how a returning consumer finds its next work. || Work queue assigns a job / Log retains ordered records / Pub/sub distributes to subscribers / Recovery depends on configured retention

A producer submits a message, the broker stores or routes it, and a consumer performs work. “Acknowledged” can refer to several points: broker receipt, durable replication, consumer receipt, or completion of the business effect. ¶ Name the point that matters. Include stable message identity, schema version, ownership, and correlation information. A broker may be healthy while every consumer rejects the payload. Observe end-to-end age and outcomes as well as enqueue rate and broker uptime. Following one message through these stages reveals which component has actually accepted responsibility at each step. || Producer submits message / Broker acknowledges defined persistence / Consumer receives record / Business effect commits before its acknowledgment

Kafka-style partitions provide ordered lanes. An offset identifies progress in one lane, and a consumer group distributes partition work among members. Order within a partition does not establish order across all partitions. ¶ When group membership changes, a partition can move to another consumer. Work near the progress-commit boundary may repeat. Choose keys for the ordering the domain needs and commit progress according to the effect contract. One very popular partition can limit the group while other consumers are idle. Adding workers does not divide that lane automatically. Separate lane ownership, processing concurrency, and completed effects when reasoning about throughput. || Key selects partition / Group assigns partition owner / Consumer processes offset 42 / Rebalance may replay uncommitted progress

A broker delivers the same record twice. A consumer can still make one local database change if it recognises the event identity and commits that recognition with the change. A later external call may have different repetition behaviour. ¶ Delivery guarantees need a boundary: are we counting broker deliveries, execution starts, or committed effects? Trace source acceptance, retained records, retries, effect commit, and progress acknowledgment. A guarantee at one link does not automatically extend across the whole path. This vocabulary becomes useful when each stage states exactly what survives a crash and what can be repeated. || Message accepted at source / Record retained or redelivered / Consumer effect commits / Progress acknowledgment defines replay behavior

A consumer acknowledges before applying an effect, then crashes. The broker may not redeliver, so the effect is missing. This placement can support at-most-once handling at the chosen boundary: zero outcomes remain possible. ¶ If the consumer applies the effect before acknowledging, a crash in between can cause redelivery. This supports at-least-once handling under the retention and recovery assumptions, but repetition must be safe. For one local database effect, store the processed event identifier and effect in the same transaction. Doing those steps separately creates another failure gap. Neither placement alone makes an unrelated external call atomic with broker progress. || Ack before effect can lose work / Effect before ack can replay / Atomic inbox protects local effect / External effects need their own contract

A stream-processing transaction can couple consumed progress and produced records inside a system that supports that operation. An exactly-once claim can be meaningful within that stated boundary and fault model. ¶ Now substitute an external email or payment provider for the produced record. Unless another protocol covers it, the transaction cannot establish one external effect. Idempotency may make repeated attempts have one relevant outcome under a key and retention policy, while executions can still start more than once. Name the records, effects, failures, and recovery covered by the guarantee. Avoid treating “exactly once” as a property that automatically crosses every connected service. || Consume input offset / Transaction couples progress and output / Broker-visible result has scoped guarantee / External HTTP effect remains outside boundary

Messages can be published in order and still commit effects out of order when consumers process them in parallel. Distinguish log order, delivery order, start order, and effect-completion order. ¶ If account changes require sequential application, coordinate by account or an equivalent ordering boundary. A later message should not be treated as applied while an earlier required change remains unresolved. Poison records need a deliberate policy: skipping restores movement but may break the sequence. Sequence numbers and gap detection can help expose that choice. State the business ordering requirement before selecting a partitioning and concurrency arrangement. || Account event sequence 10,11 / Parallel workers finish 11 first / Commit policy detects dependency / Handle gap before claiming ordered effects

A consumer finishes its local update, but its acknowledgment is lost. The broker can redeliver the record. If acknowledgment were earlier, a crash could instead lose the update. ¶ The acknowledgment tells the broker what progress it may record under its protocol. No placement makes it automatically atomic with a separate external system. Use appropriate local transactions, inbox/outbox records, provider keys, and reconciliation. Also match acknowledgment deadlines or lease extensions to expected processing time. Otherwise, healthy long-running work may be reassigned repeatedly. Identify the effect boundary first, then choose where progress can safely advance. || Effect starts / Effect commits / Consumer acknowledges / Lost acknowledgment triggers safe replay

A temporary provider outage may deserve a delayed retry. A permanently invalid address will not improve because the same payload is sent a thousand times. Classify failures and bound the attempts and waiting time. ¶ A dead-letter queue holds work needing another path, such as repair or review. It does not mean the business task is complete. Retain original identity, source version, attempt information, and a safe error reason. Give important unresolved work an owner and alerting. Replay must preserve duplicate and ordering rules. A repair process should restore valid progress rather than turn rejected records into uncontrolled new operations. || Attempt fails transiently / Backoff and retry within policy / Persistent failure quarantined / Repair and replay preserve original identity

A particular message fails every time while neighbouring messages work. It may have malformed data, an unsupported schema, or a rule violation. Such a poison message needs a different response from a transient network fault. ¶ Quarantine enough evidence to diagnose it and decide whether dependent records must wait. Avoid retaining unnecessary sensitive payloads. A software bug can also reject an entire legitimate historical version, so inspect failure patterns and samples before discarding data. Bounded retries protect resources while a review path protects the business work. Both are needed when skipping a record could leave an important operation unresolved. || Decode or validate record / Deterministic failure repeats / Quarantine with bounded evidence / Decide sequence and repair policy

The first delivery inserts processed(event_id) and increments a counter in one transaction. A second delivery encounters the same unique identity and does not repeat the increment. This is an idempotent consumer for that local effect. ¶ Keep the identity long enough for the supported replay and delivery window. If recognition is only in memory, restart loses it. If recognition commits separately from the effect, a crash can either lose or repeat the work. External providers remain outside this local transaction, so they need their own stable key or reconciliation. State what outcome is protected rather than assuming the consumer's name establishes it. || Begin local transaction / Insert unique processed-event ID / Apply business state change / Commit both or neither

Instead of storing only the latest order state, event sourcing keeps the accepted domain events as its authoritative history. Applying them in order reconstructs the current state. A snapshot can speed this work if it records the event position it represents. ¶ The history must remain interpretable as schemas change. Define corrections, replay, privacy, and retention. Rebuilding a projection must not resend real charges or emails merely because their events are read again. Auditability also depends on domain and access rules; append-only storage is not automatically a sufficient financial ledger. Choose the approach for a real need to preserve and interpret that history. || Append domain event / Fold events into current state / Snapshot records position / Replay rebuilds state without repeating effects

An order command model checks legal transitions. A customer-history view arranges the same accepted information for convenient reading. CQRS separates these responsibilities or models when their requirements differ. ¶ A projection may update the read model later, so define what a user sees immediately after a command. It may need a current-owner read, a progress indicator, or an accepted delay. CQRS can use one database and process. Event sourcing and separate services are additional choices. The extra models and synchronisation are worthwhile when they simplify actual workloads, not merely because reads and writes have different names. || Command updates authoritative model / Change feeds read projection / Query serves convenient view / Freshness contract explains projection lag

An order commits, then the process dies before publishing OrderPlaced. A transactional outbox closes this local gap by writing the order change and publication intent in the same database transaction. ¶ A relay later publishes the intent and records its progress. If it crashes after publication but before recording progress, publication can repeat. Use stable event identities and consumer duplicate handling. Monitor old pending intents and retain an appropriate recovery history. The pattern coordinates local state with the obligation to publish; it does not make every subscriber part of the original transaction or guarantee one external effect everywhere. || Transaction updates order / Same commit inserts outbox event / Relay publishes event / Crash before mark can duplicate publication

Checkout reserves stock, authorises payment, and arranges shipment across separate owners. A saga records these local steps and the actions needed if the overall workflow cannot continue. ¶ Compensation might release a reservation or request a refund. It may itself fail and need retries, and it is not always a perfect inverse: a refund can occur after costs or user-visible effects. Persist both forward and compensating progress. Distinguish uncertain, recoverable intermediate states from final outcomes. When replies are lost, reconcile with the relevant authority before assuming a step did not happen or starting it again. || Reserve inventory / Authorize payment / Shipment creation fails / Compensate and track unresolved actions

With choreography, participants react to each other's events. With orchestration, an explicit coordinator records workflow progress and chooses the next action. Both can implement the same business sequence with different ownership and visibility. ¶ Choreography can avoid one central controller but make end-to-end state harder to see. Orchestration makes retries and compensation explicit while adding a coordinator and evolving workflow definition. Neither removes participant failure. Choose according to the complexity, audit needs, and teams changing the process. Carry a shared correlation identity through either design so an operator can follow one business operation across its steps. || Participant events drive next action / Orchestrator alternatively issues commands / Both need durable participant outcomes / Observe one correlated business workflow

A workflow pauses for human approval, then resumes after a process restart. A workflow engine can persist progress, timers, and retry decisions under its supported programming model. ¶ Activities still call outside systems. If an activity's effect succeeds but its completion record is lost, it may run again. Understand the engine's replay, determinism, versioning, activity identity, and cancellation rules. Use stable effect identities and reconciliation where necessary. The engine makes durable coordination easier; the application must still define correct transitions and safe external actions. Persistent orchestration is a useful mechanism with a scope, rather than a universal exactly-once guarantee. || Workflow state persisted / Activity dispatched / Result may be uncertain after worker loss / Resume with stable activity identity

A durable job has an identity, input reference, progress state, attempt history, and an owner that can be replaced. A worker completes only if its current attempt or fencing epoch is still valid. ¶ Store large inputs separately and verify their version before processing. An old worker must not overwrite a newer attempt's output. Bound retries and expose terminal or review states when work cannot finish automatically. A process-local background task has none of these recovery properties merely because the request returned 202. The response and durable acceptance contract must agree about who will continue the work after a crash. || Persist job and input version / Worker claims attempt epoch / Work produces versioned result / Conditional completion rejects stale attempt

A schedule says a report is due at noon. The scheduler needs a stable occurrence identity, such as (schedule_id,occurrence), and an atomic way to claim or create that run. ¶ Define missed periods, clock changes, overlap, and failover. A lease can expire while its worker still runs, so protect effects with an appropriate idempotency or fencing protocol. A single scheduler process does not by itself make runs recoverable or unique across restarts. Separate deciding that an occurrence is due from safely executing its external effect. Both stages need durable identity and recovery rules. || Due occurrence identified / Atomic claim assigns epoch / Worker executes stable run ID / Expiry and recovery preserve effect identity

A consumer falls behind and later replays retained input. Replaying as fast as possible can overwhelm the same live dependencies currently serving users. Backpressure and replay need coordinated capacity limits. ¶ Give catch-up work its own rate budget, preserve source identities, and suppress unintended repeated side effects. Retention must cover the required recovery interval. Monitor oldest age and per-key blockage as well as queue length and throughput. A processor can be busy while one important operation remains stuck. Useful progress means business work reaching its defined outcome, rather than merely reading many historical messages. || Consumer capacity limited / Producer slows or queue stays bounded / Replay uses separate budget / Completion age remains observable

A local payment remains pending while the provider shows it charged. Reconciliation compares these intended and observed states and repairs the mismatch using the stable operation identity. ¶ Query and update safely instead of blindly submitting another charge. The reconciler must also be authorised, idempotent, and observable. Run it periodically and during recovery, and track unresolved discrepancies by age and owner. It is part of the normal protocol for partial failures. It supplements correct persistence and validation on the main path rather than excusing missing records that make later investigation impossible. || Local intent remains uncertain / Read provider outcome by identity / Compare with local invariant / Repair once and record evidence''')

put(17, '''Two workers use the same disk and credential source. Their processes are separate, but one disk failure or credential change can stop both. A failure domain groups parts exposed to a common cause. ¶ Map process, host, zone, dependency, and administrative correlations. Fault isolation limits how one failure spreads, for example by separating critical resource pools. The separation costs capacity and operating work, so choose it according to the harm and recovery objective. Test the shared dependencies as well as individual processes. Counting replicas without their common causes can greatly overstate the protection the design provides. || Process boundary / Shared host and disk / Zone and dependency correlation / Isolation targets an explicit failure cause

A database refuses requests during recovery but keeps all committed rows. This is a period of unavailability without demonstrated committed-data loss. A web application can show the opposite problem: it keeps replying successfully but drops writes stored only in memory. ¶ Define availability and durability independently. Specify useful eligible responses, data-loss tolerance, and the failures being covered. Timeouts, traffic failover, backups, and replication influence different parts of that contract. A general promise of reliability needs these details before it can be measured. Choose checks that establish serving behaviour and retained state rather than treating either one as evidence of both. || Request success indicator / Committed-state retention test / Recovery duration objective / Separate evidence for separate promises

Two instances each operate at 70% of their individual capacity. If one fails, the remaining instance cannot absorb both workloads at the same rate. Redundancy needs enough surviving capacity or an explicit reduced-service policy. ¶ It also needs independent failure placement, usable state, detection, and traffic movement. Shared bad configuration can disable every replica at once. Test takeover and restoration under the actual load instead of treating the second box as automatic protection. Spare resources are useful when the system can find them, direct work to them, and preserve the required state during the transition. || Two instances at 70% / One instance fails / Remaining load exceeds capacity / Headroom or shedding preserves essential service

In active-active operation, several locations or owners serve the selected function. Active-passive keeps a standby ready for takeover. The choice can differ by layer: application instances may all serve while one database leader owns writes. ¶ A passive standby can have drift or warmup problems that appear only during takeover. Multiple active owners need routing and suitable coordination or conflict rules. For either arrangement, define detection, promotion, fencing, surviving capacity, and the return after repair. A standby that has never reconstructed usable state is an untested assumption. The operating model is a complete transition procedure, not just a traffic label. || Normal serving topology / Standby or concurrent active owner / Failure transfers responsibility / Test data authority and return path

A client permits two seconds for an operation. It would be wasteful to spend three seconds waiting for a database connection before starting the query. An overall deadline bounds the useful lifetime of the request. ¶ Stage limits can cover connection setup, pool acquisition, and execution, but should fit the remaining overall time. Propagate that remaining time downstream and clean up work when possible. A timeout means the caller stopped waiting; an external write may already have occurred. Record which stage timed out so diagnosis distinguishes waiting from computation. Independent full timeouts at every layer can keep useless work alive far beyond the caller's limit. || Establish end-to-end deadline / Allocate remaining stage budgets / Cancel owned work on expiry / Reconcile uncertain remote effects

A dropped connection before a safe lookup may justify another attempt. An invalid payment amount will not improve if the same request is repeated. Classify the error before retrying. ¶ Use bounded attempts, increasing backoff, jitter, and a deadline. Repeated effects need a stable operation identity. Respect relevant retry guidance while keeping the whole business operation within its intended lifetime. Retry ownership should be clear across layers; several independently aggressive policies can multiply load. Retries are additional work spent to recover from a particular failure, so include that work in capacity and recovery planning. || Classify failure / Check safe identity and remaining time / Wait randomized bounded backoff / Retry or surface explicit outcome

Four nested layers each allow three attempts. One logical request can then create up to 3⁴ = 81 downstream attempts. During a dependency outage, this amplification can turn partial capacity into a complete collapse. ¶ Jitter spreads attempts in time but does not reduce their total by itself. Use an overall attempt budget, admission controls, and suppression of redundant work. Measure original intents separately from attempts and useful completions. An outage-related rise in requests may come from retries rather than new users. Protect capacity for successful work and recovery instead of letting repeated failures consume it all. || One intent enters four layers / Each permits three attempts / Potential downstream attempts: 81 / Limit retries across the whole path

A dependency repeatedly fails. A circuit breaker can temporarily reject new calls rather than making every caller wait for another likely failure. Common states are closed, open, and half-open, where limited probes test recovery. ¶ Define which failures count and how probes are bounded. Avoid all instances probing simultaneously after a shared timer. The breaker does not repair the dependency or decide a safe business fallback. It must not turn a failed authorisation call into a fabricated payment approval. Its useful role is controlling further resource expenditure while the application returns or performs a deliberately chosen degraded behaviour. || Closed allows calls / Failure threshold opens breaker / Open rejects quickly / Half-open probes bounded recovery

Receipt email stalls and occupies every worker. Payment authorisation now cannot obtain resources even though its provider is healthy. Bulkheads reserve or separate resources for work with different failure priorities. ¶ Dedicated pools can keep optional delivery from consuming checkout's whole allowance. Check shared limits as well: two pools may still exhaust one database or host memory. Too much partitioning can leave spare resources unused, so choose boundaries by harm and workload. Observe saturation and rejection in each pool. Isolation is effective when it covers the resource through which failure would spread, not merely when two thread-pool names exist. || Critical payment pool / Separate optional email pool / Email stalls and saturates / Payment retains its reserved capacity

A token bucket accumulates tokens at a configured rate up to a capacity. Each admitted request spends tokens. It permits a bounded burst while controlling continuing admission. A shaper may instead delay work to change its pace. ¶ For several tenants, combine fairness with protection of total service capacity. Define the identity, cost unit, burst, and failure policy. A limiter whose remote state is unavailable must reject, allow, or use a previously allocated local budget according to an explicit contract. These choices change both availability and abuse exposure. Test that path rather than assuming the limiter always has a healthy backing store. || Identify caller and operation / Consume allowed token atomically / Reject or delay excess / Enforce global and tenant budgets

Under overload, the service may stop generating recommendations while preserving order status and reconciliation. Load shedding rejects selected work early enough to keep resources available for higher-priority outcomes. ¶ Choose priorities from product and correctness requirements. Rejecting after expensive computation saves little. Return a clear overload result and bound subsequent retries. Shedding can improve useful completions while demand exceeds capacity; it cannot sustain an impossible workload forever. More capacity, less work, or a changed target may still be needed. Measure useful accepted outcomes so a drop in raw throughput is not mistaken for failure when deliberate shedding protects essential work. || Detect bounded-capacity limit / Admit essential work / Reject optional excess early / Observe goodput and fairness

A product page can remain useful without recommendations. A sale cannot safely proceed merely because an old cached count says stock exists. Graceful degradation preserves an agreed subset of useful behaviour while respecting the rules that still matter. ¶ Specify which data may be stale, which actions pause, and what the user sees. Test rarely exercised degraded paths before relying on them during an incident. Catching every error and returning a default does not establish safety. The alternative outcome needs the same clarity about permissions, freshness, and meaning as the ordinary one. || Recommendation service fails / Catalog remains authoritative / Page omits optional recommendations / Purchase invariants remain enforced

A cached help page may substitute for an unavailable content service. A default account balance cannot substitute for an unavailable ledger. A fallback is another behaviour whose meaning must be acceptable for this operation. ¶ Check its freshness, duration, access scope, and extra load. A fallback can itself overload another dependency or hide a long incident. Switching providers also changes operation identity and recovery unless their semantics are deliberately aligned. Make degraded operation visible where needed. Evaluate the substituted result as a business outcome rather than treating any returned response as successful recovery. || Primary dependency fails / Check whether substitute is valid / Serve bounded alternative or reject / Record degraded result and freshness

A process keeps restarting because its liveness check calls an unavailable shared provider. Restarting cannot repair that provider and may make recovery harder. Keep probes focused on the decision they control. ¶ Liveness asks whether the process needs restarting. Readiness can stop new traffic while ongoing work finishes. Startup probes or allowances can cover warmup. Bound checks and avoid coupling every process restart to independent external failures. A probe response is evidence about that probe, not proof that every critical operation meets its objective. Monitor real useful operations alongside these local signals. || Startup initializes required local state / Readiness admits suitable traffic / Liveness detects stuck process / Business SLIs observe actual outcomes

An incident begins at noon. Service returns at 12:20, so the interruption lasted twenty minutes. If recovery restores data only through 11:55, there is five minutes of data-loss exposure relative to the incident point. ¶ RTO is the recovery time objective. RPO is the recovery point objective, expressing acceptable loss exposure in time. Set them for the business function, then check the whole procedure: detection, decision, restoration, validation, and traffic return. Timing only a file copy misses much of the outage. A faster restore may also use older data, so evaluate the two objectives separately. || Last recoverable point: 11:55 / Incident: 12:00 / Service restored: 12:20 / RPO exposure 5 min; recovery 20 min

A backup survives the lost host, but its only decryption key was on that host. The bytes exist, yet the application cannot recover them. A useful backup needs the surrounding restoration resources too. ¶ Document credentials, keys, schema versions, retained logs, and the usable recovery environment. Restore actual backups and check business invariants, rather than relying only on job status or checksums. Keep copies separate from the failure they protect against. Live replication can repeat corruption or deletion, while historical backups can support a different recovery point. Specify which role each copy serves and test the corresponding procedure. || Backup captured / Independent storage and keys retained / Restore into clean environment / Validate application and reconciliation state

Failover moves service or write authority to a replacement. Failback returns it after repair. Both transitions need valid history, sufficient capacity, routing, and protection from conflicting owners. ¶ An old primary may need rebuilding from the new history before rejoining. Promote according to the promised data-loss policy, and fence stale writers. Client connections, DNS caches, and retries can continue using old paths after a database promotion succeeds. Test the full user-visible sequence, including return after repair. Recovery is incomplete if traffic looks restored but a later old owner can reintroduce incompatible writes. || Detect and choose promotion / Fence old authority / Route and validate service / Rebuild old owner before safe failback

Copies in separate zones can survive some local incidents. Separate regions cover other failures but add propagation delay, transfer cost, data-placement concerns, and recovery work. ¶ Check dependencies that remain shared. A global credential change or configuration fault can still affect all regions. Plan capacity for surviving traffic and catch-up work, not just the normal split. Select placement according to named failure and recovery goals. Geographic distance is one part of independence; the authority protocol and operational dependencies determine whether the locations can actually provide the promised protection. || Host failure / Zone failure / Regional dependency failure / Each requires a deliberately independent boundary

Begin a fault exercise with a question: if a worker pauses past its lease, can its old completion overwrite a new result? Then choose a controlled pause and observe the specified outcome. ¶ Chaos engineering tests such hypotheses under bounded faults. Define the affected scope, stop conditions, observations, recovery owner, and environment appropriate to the risk. Random process killing without a hypothesis can cause harm without clarifying a mechanism. Increase scope only when justified. A passing drill provides evidence for that scenario and configuration; it does not prove every future failure harmless. || State reliability hypothesis / Inject bounded fault / Observe invariant and recovery / Stop and repair when limits are exceeded

Suppose optional calls consume most of a request's deadline and cause most failures. Removing them from the critical path may improve users' experience more directly than adding another region. ¶ Use SLO and error-budget evidence to choose reliability work. Keep important low-volume operations visible: their failures can disappear inside a large denominator dominated by cheap successful reads. Segment indicators where the harm differs. More redundancy has both cost and complexity, so evaluate the mechanism it improves. An error budget is a decision aid, rather than permission to ignore a severe failure merely because it affects few events. || Measure meaningful SLI / Compare budget consumption / Locate highest-impact failure mode / Choose justified reliability investment

A slow provider holds connections. Waiting requests accumulate, callers retry, and the extra work overloads previously healthy components. This feedback creates a cascading failure. ¶ Break the actual resource path with deadlines, bounded admission, retry budgets, isolation, and meaningful degradation. Keep capacity for recovery. A breaker that sends every failed request to one fallback can start another cascade. Trace both the original path and the recovery path under correlated failure. Testing each dependency in isolation can miss the interaction that makes a modest initial slowdown become a system-wide outage. || Provider slows / Connections and queues accumulate / Retries multiply offered work / Admission and isolation break the loop

After an incident, reconstruct what happened, which conditions allowed it, how it was detected, and what helped or delayed recovery. Stopping at “human error” leaves unanswered why one action could affect every replica. ¶ A useful analysis connects corrective work to observed mechanisms. Scoped credentials can reduce exposure; tested restores can improve recovery; bounded retries can limit amplification. Give each action an owner and evidence of completion. Avoid adding alerts without a useful response. The aim is to reduce recurrence or harm by changing the system and its operating conditions, rather than attaching blame to the person closest to the trigger. || Reconstruct evidence timeline / Identify contributing system conditions / Assign concrete corrective work / Verify the changed failure behavior''')
